Yes — give them the full model number KGN34NL3AG, and ideally the E-Nr and FD number from the appliance rating plate. Those let Bosch/an engineer identify the exact version, age/batch, and correct compressor control parts.

For Bosch fridge-freezers, the rating plate is commonly inside the fridge compartment, usually on a side wall near the salad drawers, or occasionally behind/near a drawer. It may show something like:

• E-Nr: KGN34NL3AG/xx
• FD: xxxx
• Z-Nr: xxxxxx (serial number)

The `/xx` after the model is important, so take a clear photo of the whole label if possible.

When booking, say something along these lines:

> Bosch KGN34NL3AG/[suffix], FD [number]. Intermittent compressor start failure: there is a click at the rear, a very brief hum, then it stops and retries every few minutes. The box/module on the side of the compressor becomes very hot, and the freezer temperature rises/food starts softening. It works again intermittently, including after cooling down. It happens on more than one wall socket.

Also tell them:

• when it first started happening;
• how often it happens and how long it lasts;
• whether any error code, alarm light, or high-temperature alarm appears;
• whether the fridge compartment also gets warm;
• that there is no burning smell and no circuit breaker/RCD trip;
• whether the 20–30 minute power-off test lets it restart normally, and for how long it then keeps running.

A short video capturing the click, brief hum, and compressor area is often very useful, especially because intermittent faults can disappear by the time an engineer arrives.
